I've been building software since my computer science degree at the Faculty of Sciences of Tunis, and I've kept shipping since.

Freelancing on Upwork taught me what clients pay for. They judged the result: did the product work, did it launch on time, did it help the business. Ten-plus projects and clients in eight countries later, I still judge my work that way.

At Metis I saw the same thing at scale. Metis is a B2B platform that travel agencies use to book flights. When I cut steps from the booking flow and ran supplier calls in parallel, our end-to-end booking tests finished 3x faster. When I built the NDC booking split for Air France-KLM, Metis became the first technology provider to offer it, and agencies could keep customers who wanted to change flights instead of sending them to the airline. I now lead the flight module team there and mentor junior developers.

Outside Metis, I build AI tools and agent workflows with Claude and MCP, and I'm working on Huginn, an open-source API that aggregates airline suppliers. I'm also doing a research master's in information systems at ISI.
